WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.277 --> 00:00:01.860
JOANNA SMITH: This
week, we're talking

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:04.530
about strategy guides,
cheat codes, and graphics

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:06.120
for Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.640
I'm Joanna, and welcome
to This Week in Cloud,

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.690
where we serve you the
lowest latency news.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.100
Moving to the
cloud is important,

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.220
but so is thinking
critically about which

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:20.130
parts of your business it
actually makes sense to move.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.860
To guide you, we've got
a post for all the CIOs

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.080
is currently wrestling
with this question.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:28.020
In it, will bust your myths
about migrating and detail

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:31.710
how it's possible to decouple
dependencies for more agility,

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.500
without adding
unnecessary complexity.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.150
Like that sentence.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.130
Virtual display devices
for Chrome enterprise

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:39.900
are now generally
available, meaning

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.390
you can add a virtual
display device to any VM

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:43.410
on Google Cloud.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.770
This gives your VM video
graphics array capabilities

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:50.430
without having to use GPUs,
which can be powerful, but also

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:51.510
expensive.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.660
Get the full scoop
on the Cloud Blog.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.180
One of the greatest
challenges customers

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.800
face when onboarding
in the cloud

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.650
is how to control and protect
their assets while letting

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.420
their users deploy
resources securely.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:06.750
So we created this
series of posts

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.960
to show you how to write your
security policies as code, once

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.470
and for all, and to
apply them both before

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.740
and after you deploy resources
in your GCP environment.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.580
The first post is about using
Forseti Config Validator.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.745
It's no secret that Google
Cloud offers a lot of products.

00:01:24.745 --> 00:01:26.370
To help you understand
the differences,

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:28.450
Greg Wilson has a cheat sheet.

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:29.280
It's simple.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.670
All of the approximately
222 products we offer

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:35.160
explained in four words or less.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:38.447
Let it be your light when
looking to the cloud.

00:01:38.447 --> 00:01:40.530
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:42.850
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:44.500
Have any thoughts or ideas?

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:49.340
Leave us a comment or tweet us,
and we'll see you in the cloud.

